AI Prototype Summary






Figure 3: A screenshot of the HTML website interface showcasing more functionality than the prompt intended.



It did a phenomenal job at creating a functional website that was able to control the LED and change the resolution of the temperature readings. The AI was able to generate code that was clean and efficient, with minimal errors for HTML. It also added some extra features that made sense for an overall project, but it made some assumptions that weren’t so accurate. For example, it added a “Read Temperature” button that was not necessary since the temperature is read automatically and displayed on the web page. When I fed it a bit more of my code, it only made mild changes to the background color and overall formatting.
When I gave it the following prompt:

Write me a C function to carry out a SPI transaction to retrieve a temperature reading from a DS1722 sensor. Make use of CMSIS libraries for the STM32L432KC.

It responded with the following code which is a very good starting point for the SPI transaction to retrieve a temperature reading from a DS1722 sensor. The code is well-structured and makes use of CMSIS libraries for the STM32L432KC, providing a clear implementation of the SPI communication process. There are a lot of the same ideas that I used in my code, but it is a bit more structured and easier to read. It also includes error handling and comments for clarity. Overall, it is a solid implementation that can be used as a reference for SPI communication with the DS1722 sensor. Unfortunately it didn’t synthesize as it assumed a little bit more about the project than I had provided, but it was still a good starting point for the SPI transaction to retrieve a temperature reading from a DS1722 sensor.






NoteAI-Generated DS1722 SPI Function





#include "stm32l4xx_hal.h"
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;

/* DS1722 Register Addresses */
#define DS1722_READ_LSB_TEMP   0x01
#define DS1722_READ_MSB_TEMP   0x02
#define DS1722_READ_CONFIG     0x00
#define DS1722_WRITE_CONFIG    0x80

/* Resolution Bitmasks (Config Bits 3..1) */
typedef enum {
    DS1722_RES_8BIT  = 0x00, /* 1.0°C resolution   (Conversion time ~75ms)  */
    DS1722_RES_9BIT  = 0x02, /* 0.5°C resolution   (Conversion time ~150ms) */
    DS1722_RES_10BIT = 0x04, /* 0.25°C resolution  (Conversion time ~300ms) */
    DS1722_RES_11BIT = 0x06, /* 0.125°C resolution (Conversion time ~600ms) */
    DS1722_RES_12BIT = 0x08  /* 0.0625°C resolution(Conversion time ~1200ms)*/
} DS1722_Resolution;

/**
* @brief  Reads the temperature from a DS1722 SPI sensor.
* @param  hspi     Pointer to SPI_HandleTypeDef structure (e.g., &hspi1)
* @param  cs_port  GPIO Port connected to DS1722 CE/CS pin (e.g., GPIOA)
* @param  cs_pin   GPIO Pin connected to DS1722 CE/CS pin (e.g., GPIO_PIN_4)
* @param  temp_out Pointer to float variable where temperature in °C will be stored
* @retval HAL_StatusTypeDef (HAL_OK on success)
*/
HAL_StatusTypeDef DS1722_ReadTemperature(SPI_HandleTypeDef *hspi, 
                                        GPIO_TypeDef *cs_port, 
                                        uint16_t cs_pin, 
                                        float *temp_out) 
{
    uint8_t tx_buf[3];
    uint8_t rx_buf[3] = {0};
    HAL_StatusTypeDef status;

    if (temp_out == NULL) {
        return HAL_ERROR;
    }

    // Step 1: Prepare Address and Dummy Bytes
    // DS1722 address byte bit 7 = 0 for Read operations.
    // Address 0x01 reads LSB, continuous clocking automatically reads MSB at 0x02.
    tx_buf[0] = DS1722_READ_LSB_TEMP;
    tx_buf[1] = 0x00; // Dummy byte to clock out LSB byte
    tx_buf[2] = 0x00; // Dummy byte to clock out MSB byte

    // Step 2: Assert Chip Enable / Chip Select (DS1722 CE is Active High)
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(cs_port, cs_pin, GPIO_PIN_SET);

    // Step 3: Perform 3-byte SPI Transaction (Address + LSB + MSB)
    status = HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive(hspi, tx_buf, rx_buf, 3, HAL_MAX_DELAY);

    // Step 4: De-assert Chip Enable
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(cs_port, cs_pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET);

    if (status != HAL_OK) {
        return status;
    }

    // Step 5: Convert Raw Sensor Output to °C
    // rx_buf[1] holds LSB (fractional part), rx_buf[2] holds MSB (integer/sign part)
    uint8_t raw_lsb = rx_buf[1];
    int8_t  raw_msb = (int8_t)rx_buf[2]; // Signed integer for negative temperatures

    // Combine raw registers: MSB is integer portion, LSB top 4 bits are fractional (1/16ths = 0.0625°C)
    float temperature = (float)raw_msb;
    temperature += (float)(raw_lsb &gt;&gt; 4) * 0.0625f;

    *temp_out = temperature;
    return HAL_OK;
}









